### What is the check-then-act race?

Every request reads stock=1, all decide to take it, all decrement. The result: overselling to multiple buyers.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

public class RaceCondition {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("The race:");
        System.out.println("  Thread A: reads stock=1 -> decides to buy");
        System.out.println("  Thread B: reads stock=1 -> decides to buy");
        System.out.println("  Thread A: stock = stock - 1 = 0");
        System.out.println("  Thread B: stock = stock - 1 = -1 (OVERSOLD!)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Fix: make check-and-decrement ATOMIC");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
The race:
  Thread A: reads stock=1 -> decides to buy
  Thread B: reads stock=1 -> decides to buy
  Thread A: stock = stock - 1 = 0
  Thread B: stock = stock - 1 = -1 (OVERSOLD!)

Fix: make check-and-decrement ATOMIC
```

### Rung 1: In-JVM primitives (single instance only)

AtomicInteger.compareAndSet atomically takes the last unit only if it is still one.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class InJvmLocking {
    private static final AtomicInteger stock = new AtomicInteger(1);

    public static boolean tryBuy() {
        // Atomic: takes the last unit ONLY if stock is still 1
        return stock.compareAndSet(1, 0);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Buyer 1: " + (tryBuy() ? "GOT IT" : "SOLD OUT"));
        System.out.println("Buyer 2: " + (tryBuy() ? "GOT IT" : "SOLD OUT"));
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Buyer 1: GOT IT
Buyer 2: SOLD OUT

Limitation: works within ONE JVM only.
Two instances -> each has its own AtomicInteger -> both can sell.
```

### Rung 2: DB optimistic locking (across instances)

A version column guards the UPDATE. If another transaction already decremented, the version no longer matches.

```sql
-- Optimistic locking: conditional update with version
UPDATE stock
SET qty = qty - 1, version = version + 1
WHERE product_id = 'FD-DIWALI-50' AND version = 3 AND qty > 0;
-- If another thread already decremented: version != 3 -> 0 rows updated
```

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

public class DbOptimisticLocking {
    // Simpler: conditional atomic decrement (no version needed)
    // UPDATE stock SET qty = qty - 1 WHERE product_id = ? AND qty > 0
    // Only one thread succeeds when qty = 1

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("DB optimistic locking:");
        System.out.println("  Thread A: UPDATE SET qty=qty-1 WHERE qty>0 -> 1 row updated");
        System.out.println("  Thread B: UPDATE SET qty=qty-1 WHERE qty>0 -> 0 rows updated");
        System.out.println("  Thread B: 'SOLD OUT'");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Works across ALL instances sharing one database");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
DB optimistic locking:
  Thread A: UPDATE SET qty=qty-1 WHERE qty>0 -> 1 row updated
  Thread B: UPDATE SET qty=qty-1 WHERE qty>0 -> 0 rows updated
  Thread B: 'SOLD OUT'

Works across ALL instances sharing one database
```

### Rung 3: Redis distributed lock (no shared DB row)

When the resource is not a database row, use SET NX PX so only one caller wins.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import org.springframework.data.redis.core.StringRedisTemplate;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.UUID;

public class RedisDistributedLock {

    public static boolean tryAcquire(StringRedisTemplate redis, String key) {
        String token = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        Boolean acquired = redis.opsForValue()
                .setIfAbsent(key, token, Duration.ofSeconds(10));
        if (Boolean.TRUE.equals(acquired)) {
            System.out.println("Lock acquired: " + key);
            return true;
        }
        System.out.println("Lock NOT acquired: " + key + " (someone else has it)");
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Redis SET NX PX:");
        System.out.println("  NX: set only if key absent -> only first caller wins");
        System.out.println("  PX 10000: TTL 10 seconds -> crashed holder's lock expires");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Cautions:");
        System.out.println("  TTL too short -> expires mid-operation -> two holders");
        System.out.println("  Prefer DB constraint over Redis lock when possible");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Redis SET NX PX:
  NX: set only if key absent -> only first caller wins
  PX 10000: TTL 10 seconds -> crashed holder's lock expires

Cautions:
  TTL too short -> expires mid-operation -> two holders
  Prefer DB constraint over Redis lock when possible
```

### Rung 4: Single-consumer queue (no locks needed)

Every request goes onto a queue. One consumer processes them one at a time. Zero contention.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

public class QueueSerialization {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Single-consumer queue:");
        System.out.println("  All purchase requests -> Kafka topic (1 partition)");
        System.out.println("  One consumer processes sequentially");
        System.out.println("  No contention, no locks, no races");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Benefits:");
        System.out.println("  - Simplest correctness story");
        System.out.println("  - Natural fairness (first-come-first-served)");
        System.out.println("  - Backpressure and audit trail built in");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Best for: extreme flash-sale contention");
        System.out.println("  Even DB locks thrash at 10,000 concurrent requests");
        System.out.println("  Queue absorbs the spike, consumer processes calmly");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Single-consumer queue:
  All purchase requests -> Kafka topic (1 partition)
  One consumer processes sequentially
  No contention, no locks, no races
```

### How do you choose the right rung?

| Rung | Scope | When to use |
|---|---|---|
| In-JVM (AtomicInteger) | Single instance | One server, no scale-out |
| DB locking | Multiple instances, one DB | Shared database, moderate contention |
| Redis lock | Multiple instances, no shared row | External resource, non-DB entity |
| Queue | Extreme contention | Flash sale, thousands of concurrent claims |

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

public class ChoosingRung {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Decision guide:");
        System.out.println("  Single instance? -> AtomicInteger CAS");
        System.out.println("  Multiple instances, shared DB? -> Conditional UPDATE");
        System.out.println("  No lockable DB row? -> Redis SET NX PX");
        System.out.println("  Extreme contention? -> Queue to single consumer");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Principle: cheapest technique whose scope matches deployment");
        System.out.println("  Climb only when scaling out breaks the current rung");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Decision guide:
  Single instance? -> AtomicInteger CAS
  Multiple instances, shared DB? -> Conditional UPDATE
  No lockable DB row? -> Redis SET NX PX
  Extreme contention? -> Queue to single consumer

Principle: cheapest technique whose scope matches deployment
```

### What is the WRONG pattern?

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

public class WrongPattern {
    // WRONG: read-then-write (not atomic)
    // if (stock > 0) { stock--; } // Two threads both see stock > 0

    // WRONG: synchronized across multiple instances
    // synchronized (this) { stock--; } // Each JVM has its own lock!

    // CORRECT: AtomicInteger.compareAndSet for single JVM
    // CORRECT: UPDATE stock SET qty=qty-1 WHERE qty>0 for multi-instance

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: if (stock > 0) stock-- // not atomic, race condition");
        System.out.println("WRONG: synchronized across JVMs // each JVM has own lock");
        System.out.println("CORRECT: atomic CAS or conditional DB UPDATE");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: if (stock > 0) stock-- // not atomic, race condition
WRONG: synchronized across JVMs // each JVM has own lock
CORRECT: atomic CAS or conditional DB UPDATE
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Prefer database constraints over Redis locks. The conditional UPDATE is often the simplest correct solution for database-backed resources.

```text
Senior guidelines:
  [x] Always make check-and-act ATOMIC (never read-then-write)
  [x] Single JVM: AtomicInteger.compareAndSet
  [x] Multi-instance: conditional UPDATE (cheapest, most robust)
  [x] Non-DB resource: Redis SET NX PX (with caution on TTL)
  [x] Extreme contention: single-consumer queue
  [x] Prefer DB constraint over Redis lock where possible
  [x] Monitor per-shard stock levels for early warning
```
